  "description": "Boarding, alighting and capacity (G1b, ARCHITECTURE decision 35) on the line a(0,1)-b(1,1)-...-g(6,1), dead ends at both ends; Alpha(1,0) has platform b, Beta(3,0) d, Gamma(5,0) f. Main (Alpha, Beta, Gamma) runs all day; its round trip leaving Alpha at T is Alpha T, Beta T+2 to T+3, Gamma T+5 to T+7 (turning round), Beta T+9 to T+10, Alpha T+12 (turning round; the service ends). Blue, one car (352 passengers at most: 320 rated x 1.1), is on Main. Alpha (residential, 1,000,000 a day) fills its queue of 4000 within a minute of 07:50; Beta (office, 20,000) and Gamma (office, 1,000). The line runs no train until 07:59, then one: Blue leaves Alpha at 480 taking the farthest first (all 127 for Gamma, then 225 of the Beta groups, earliest first); the 3648 left behind are refused. As a train leaves a stop, those riding to it get off (arrived), then it takes on passengers for its line and direction whose destination it calls at before it next turns round. The 225 get off at Beta at 483, the 127 at Gamma at 487, where Blue turns round and takes 19 for Alpha; at Beta (490) it has room for 333 of the 455 waiting for Alpha (122 refused). Back at Alpha (492) the service ends; the round trip takes 14 minutes (8 travelling, 1 + 1 at Beta, 2 + 2 at the ends), so the line sends Blue out again at 494, with 352 for Beta. A line's train cannot have its service stopped (trainOnLine); taken off the line it finishes the trip as an ordinary service, and when that service is stopped at Beta at 497 its 352 riders are abandoned. Every count is from the independent G1a/G1b calculator (docs: GoldenScenarios/README.md); released = waiting + riding + arrived + overflowed + abandoned at every station.",
